class Student {
    String name;
    int[] marks;
    Student(String name, int[] marks) {
        this.name = name;
        this.marks = marks;
    }
}
public class StudentResults {
    public static void calculateAverages(Student[] students) {
        for (Student s : students) {
            int total = 0;
            for (int mark : s.marks) {
                total += mark;
            }
            double avg = (double) total / s.marks.length;
            System.out.println("Student: " + s.name + " | Average: " + avg);
        }
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student[] studentArray = {
            new Student("Alice", new int[]{85, 90, 88}),
            new Student("Bob", new int[]{70, 65, 75})
        };
        calculateAverages(studentArray);
    }
}
Explanation:
An array of objects (Student[]) stores multiple student records. The method calculateAverages() accepts the array and processes each object. This modular approach supports code reuse, clarity, and object-oriented handling of structured data.

